claim_quote:
A (\emph{generalized}) \emph{simplicial complex} $X$ on a vertex set $V$ is simply a family of subsets of $V$, closed under inclusion. In particular, $\ver(X):=\{x\in V\colon \{x\}\in X\}$ is called the set of \emph{actual vertices} of $X$. If $u\in V\setminus \ver(X)$, we say that $u$ is a \emph{ghost vertex} of $X$. A set $A\in X$ is said to be a \emph{face} of $X$, and the dimension of a face $A$ is $\dim(A)=|A|-1$. The \emph{dimension} $\dim(X)$ of a complex $X$ is the maximum dimension of a face in $X$. For a given vertex $v\in \ver(X)$, the \emph{deletion} and \emph{link} subcomplexes of $X$ at the vertex $v$ is defined by $\del(X;v):=\{S\in X\colon v\notin S\}$ and $\lk(X;v):=\{T\in X\colon v\notin T\;\textnormal{and}\;T\cup \{v\}\in X\}$. For convenience, if $u$ is a ghost vertex, we set $\lk(X;u):=\del(X;u)=\{\emptyset\}$. A vertex $v\in V$ is said to be a \emph{cone vertex} of $X$ if
$\lk(X;v)=\del(X;v)$, and denote by $V^{\circ}$, the set of all non-cone vertices in $X$. Unless stated otherwise, by a vertex of a simplicial complex, we mean an actual vertex of it.

\begin{definition}\label{defn:dreg}
Let $\XE$ be the family of all finite simplicial complexes. We define a function $\theta\colon \XE\to \N_0$ by $\theta(X):=0$ if $V(X)^{\circ}=\emptyset$, and
$$\theta(X):=\underset{v\in V(X)^{\circ}}{\min}\{\max\{\theta(\del(X;v)),\theta(\lk(X;v))+1\}\}$$
whenever $V(X)^{\circ}\neq \emptyset$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}
$\theta(\lk(X;v))\leq \theta(\del(X;v))$ for every vertex $v$ in $X$.
\end{theorem}

